{"type":"assistant.message","data":{"messageId":"90ccc0dc-f9c8-475d-8b24-947c3f492650","originatingMessageId":"73c76ac7-38c1-4a1e-a8f8-81f01d04ec99","model":"claude-sonnet-5.5","providerId":"copilot","content":"","toolRequests":[{"toolCallId":"toolu_01FxiMQRKQhA8REgexuckYm4","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /Users/dwahl/Scripts/pantoken/formats/components/src && grep -rn '\"ai-gradient\"\\|name: \"ai-gradient' . ../../../plugins/pantoken/*/src 2>/dev/null | head; sed -n 1,60p utilities/position/index.ts | grep -n \"position-\\|@modifier\" | head; grep -n \"@modifier\" utilities/stacking/index.ts utilities/layout/index.ts utilities/mask/index.ts | cut -c1-160 | head -30; sed -n 1,60p rules/prose/index.ts","description":"Check class names, prose, ai-gradient collision"},"type":"function","intentionSummary":"Check class names, prose, ai-gradient collision","toolTitle":"Running command"}],"interactionId":"b4f03ceb-0b21-44ca-9a3e-694263c338bd","turnId":"9","reasoningOpaque":"REDACTED","reasoningText":"I'm working out whether to wrap examples in html fences when stripping metadata, checking how backticks need escaping inside the css template literal so existing comment syntax still parses correctly.\n\nI also need to confirm how resolveExampleTargets handles the \"-automatica11y\" flag—whether unflagged examples default to the docs audience and how mixing a plain @example with a flagged one affects what shows up in documentation versus Canvas output.\n\nNow I'm moving on to drafting actual fixture content for each case, making sure examples use the \"instui-\" prefix convention and are realistic enough to pass checks, like a truncate example using a paragraph with max-line styling and an inline width constraint.\n\nFor overflow, I'm writing a scrollable region with tabindex, a region role, and an accessible label so keyboard users can reach it, noting that any scrollable container needs either tabindex plus a role and name, or a focusable child element. For spacing, I'm sketching a simple padding/margin example and reminding myself the finding should clarify that spacing utilities are purely visual and shouldn't be relied on to convey meaningful grouping.\n\n6. responsive hidden/show classes: @a11y concern—hiding content at breakpoints removes it from the accessibility tree too, so avoid hiding the only way to complete a task or hiding large-screen info on small screens. I'm using `instui-hidden-max-sm` and `instui-show-min-sm` classes with an example showing a full description on larger screens and a short one for smaller ones.\n7. position: `--position-rel...\n\nContinuing through positioning, stacking order, cursor, and mask overlay utilities—each with an accessibility caveat: positioning shouldn't desync visual order from DOM/focus order, absolutely positioned content shouldn't cover focused elements, pointer cursors don't make elements operable (use real buttons/links), and mask overlays are purely visual so interactive content underneath needs inert/aria-busy handling when masked.\n\nI'm also sketching examples for transition and visual-debug utilities, and thinking through how the base example's `<html>` wrapper would get stripped when rendered inside the audit page's `<main>`, leaving just the inner link markup intact.\n\nNow I'm considering prose and ai-gradient entries — checking whether \"ai-gradient\" collides with an existing core component name, which would break the example-collection logic requiring unique entries, and flagging segmented-control.segment as something to verify too.\n\nI still need to dedupe overlapping names between COMPONENTS and UTILITIES (like icon and screen-reader-content), confirm \"maskglobal\" doesn't clash with the \"mask\" component, and grep for position/stacking/mask classes plus prose content to be sure the ai-gradient collision is real.","rte":true,"apiCallId":"msg_011CgVAMued6isV6DWAz3iqs","reasoningBlocks":{"provider":"anthropic-messages","blocks":[{"signature":"","thinking":"I'm working out whether to wrap examples in html fences when stripping metadata, checking how backticks need escaping inside the css template literal so existing comment syntax still parses correctly.\n\nI also need to confirm how resolveExampleTargets handles the \"-automatica11y\" flag—whether unflagged examples default to the docs audience and how mixing a plain @example with a flagged one affects what shows up in documentation versus Canvas output.\n\nNow I'm moving on to drafting actual fixture content for each case, making sure examples use the \"instui-\" prefix convention and are realistic enough to pass checks, like a truncate example using a paragraph with max-line styling and an inline width constraint.\n\nFor overflow, I'm writing a scrollable region with tabindex, a region role, and an accessible label so keyboard users can reach it, noting that any scrollable container needs either tabindex plus a role and name, or a focusable child element. For spacing, I'm sketching a simple padding/margin example and reminding myself the finding should clarify that spacing utilities are purely visual and shouldn't be relied on to convey meaningful grouping.\n\n6. responsive hidden/show classes: @a11y concern—hiding content at breakpoints removes it from the accessibility tree too, so avoid hiding the only way to complete a task or hiding large-screen info on small screens. I'm using `instui-hidden-max-sm` and `instui-show-min-sm` classes with an example showing a full description on larger screens and a short one for smaller ones.\n7. position: `--position-rel...\n\nContinuing through positioning, stacking order, cursor, and mask overlay utilities—each with an accessibility caveat: positioning shouldn't desync visual order from DOM/focus order, absolutely positioned content shouldn't cover focused elements, pointer cursors don't make elements operable (use real buttons/links), and mask overlays are purely visual so interactive content underneath needs inert/aria-busy handling when masked.\n\nI'm also sketching examples for transition and visual-debug utilities, and thinking through how the base example's `<html>` wrapper would get stripped when rendered inside the audit page's `<main>`, leaving just the inner link markup intact.\n\nNow I'm considering prose and ai-gradient entries — checking whether \"ai-gradient\" collides with an existing core component name, which would break the example-collection logic requiring unique entries, and flagging segmented-control.segment as something to verify too.\n\nI still need to dedupe overlapping names between COMPONENTS and UTILITIES (like icon and screen-reader-content), confirm \"maskglobal\" doesn't clash with the \"mask\" component, and grep for position/stacking/mask classes plus prose content to be sure the ai-gradient collision is real.\n\n","type":"thinking"}]}},"id":"5ec5995a-29dd-4730-91a6-8fcd6e825b1a","timestamp":"2026-10-28T19:09:38.280Z","parentId":"62337fda-c5f2-4c7d-a60d-26a675bd52a7"}
